nodes.instruction_excerpt:
Read the paper and produce a short overview: the paper title and a one-paragraph
summary of the abstract.

Paper:
{{paper_content}}

Return JSON:
{
  "title": "",
  "abstract_summary": ""
}

Re-read the paper and pull out the main section headings in order.
Paper:
{{paper_content}}

Return JSON: {"section_headings": ["Introduction", "Methods", ...]}

Look at the question stem ONLY. Do not look at the four answer options yet.
Question:
{{question}}
{{question_type_instruction}}

Pick 3-5 keywords or short phrases that best characterize what we should
search for in the paper. Return JSON: {"keywords": ["...", "..."]}

Determine whether this is a single-answer (SA-MCQ) or multiple-answer (MA-MCQ)
question, and state the exact focus of the question stem (the "core claim"
the options are testing).
Question: {{question}}
Runner-provided hint: {{question_type}}

Return JSON:
{
  "question_type": "SA-MCQ or MA-MCQ",
  "core_claim": ""
}

Inspect the question for negation words such as NOT, incorrect, wrong,
false, except. If present, note this explicitly so that the downstream
evaluation can invert polarity.
Question: {{question}}
Return: {"has_negation": "yes/no", "negation_term": ""}

## Context
Keywords: {{question_keywords}}
Core claim: {{question_class}}
Paper:
{{paper_content}}

## Task
Locate up to 5 evidence snippets in the paper that are most relevant to the
core claim. Each snippet must be a paragraph copied **VERBATIM** from the
paper. If fewer than 5 strong snippets exist, return only those.

Return JSON:
{
  "evidence_snippets": [
    {"text": "...", "matched_keyword": "...", "reason": "why this is relevant"}
  ]
}

Review the evidence we collected and decide whether it is strong enough
to discriminate among the four options.
Evidence: {{evidence}}
Question: {{question}}

If the evidence is weak or one-sided, list what is missing. Otherwise
confirm sufficiency. Return your assessment as structured JSON.

Judge option A only. Make this verdict INDEPENDENT of any other option.
Question: {{question}}
Evidence: {{evidence}}
Negation note: {{negation_flag}}

Return JSON:
{"verdict": "supported / contradicted / not_established", "reason": "", "evidence_text": ""}

Judge option B only. Independent verdict.
Question: {{question}}
Evidence: {{evidence}}
Return JSON:
{"verdict": "supported / contradicted / not_established", "reason": "", "evidence_text": ""}

Judge option C only. Independent verdict.
Question: {{question}}
Evidence: {{evidence}}
Return JSON:
{"verdict": "supported / contradicted / not_established", "reason": "", "evidence_text": ""}

Judge option D only. Independent verdict.
Question: {{question}}
Evidence: {{evidence}}
Return JSON:
{"verdict": "supported / contradicted / not_established", "reason": "", "evidence_text": ""}

This is a multiple-answer question (typically 2-3 correct).
Current independent judgments:
  A: {{judgment_A}}
  B: {{judgment_B}}
  C: {{judgment_C}}
  D: {{judgment_D}}

Count how many are currently "supported" across A-D. If
fewer than 2, reconsider the non-selected options with a
more lenient reading of the evidence ({{evidence}}). If
already 2-3, restate the existing verdicts.

Return JSON:
{
  "selected_options": [],
  "selected_count": 0,
  "reasoning": ""
}

Produce the final answer for this multi-answer question.
Question: {{question}}
Per-option judgments:
  A: {{judgment_A}}
  B: {{judgment_B}}
  C: {{judgment_C}}
  D: {{judgment_D}}
Recheck summary: {{recheck_summary}}
Negation note: {{negation_flag}}
{{question_type_instruction}}

Select the ONE correct answer.
Question: {{question}}
Per-option judgments:
  A: {{judgment_A}}
  B: {{judgment_B}}
  C: {{judgment_C}}
  D: {{judgment_D}}
Negation note: {{negation_flag}}
{{question_type_instruction}}

Question type is ambiguous; pick whichever option(s) the
evidence most clearly supports.
Question: {{question}}
Judgments: A:{{judgment_A}} B:{{judgment_B}} C:{{judgment_C}} D:{{judgment_D}}
{{question_type_instruction}}
